TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R13","met":"partial","where":"Contents"},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Altitude justification is inconsistent: a higher altitude gives lower resolution, yet this is called desirable. No swath or ground sample distance calculation supports the 600 km choice."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The claim that the initial RAAN indicates the launch facility location is unsupported and does not match standard orbit-element meaning."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mt Pleasant (about 42°S) is described as selected for a latitude close to the poles, which is a weak and partly incorrect rationale."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table duplicates access 14 as access 15, and the statistics say 15 accesses while the list is labelled with a '----' row, so the count is inconsistent."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Vega E is used as the launch vehicle though not yet flown; its 2024 first-launch claim is unsourced and conflicts with the VV18 rideshare plan."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A sentence is truncated, and the drag-sail description is incomplete. The EOL strategy has no decay time or delta-V analysis despite stated STK runs to 2030."},{"where":"Mission Design / Orbit","issue":"The descending pass is described as constantly in sunlight, which overstates the sunlit conditions for a sun-synchronous orbit."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Wikipedia is cited as [6], which the brief prohibits. Launch-table citations [49]-[74] are not in the bibliography, and [10] is reused for two different sources."}],"overall":"A broad, mostly complete space-segment design with appropriate STK support and a clear mission rationale. Weaknesses include contradictory altitude logic, an unflown launch vehicle, an access-table error, an incomplete EOL analysis, unnumbered figures and tables, and Wikipedia use with mismatched citations. Quantitative justification and reference integrity need substantial improvement.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Mission purpose, need, single-spacecraft choice with reasons, and user groups are all addressed with supporting context and some sourced statistics. The operator is not clearly identified, and the customer description is general. Funding discussion is speculative. Overall a solid, referenced introduction with minor gaps.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"All major segments are covered, with an orbit table, STK access data, ground track images and an EOL option. However, the altitude logic is contradictory, the Vega E is unflown yet is used without caveat, and the RAAN explanation is unjustified. The ground station is at about 42°S, not near the pole. There is little quantitative support for swath, resolution or EOL, and STK files are not evidenced.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Design / Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The initial value indicates the location of the launch facility."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"A very high altitude would yield low resolution images which is also desirable."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"a ground station is selected whose latitude will be frequented by the satellite."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"utilising a large surface area to"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarises the configuration and notes launch-vehicle risk, which is useful. However, it does not restate key quantitative results such as the 97.79° inclination or the access statistics. Several claims are generic or overstated, such as bleaching being minimised or solved.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"hopefully contribute to the prevention of coral bleaching within the Great Barrier Reef and elsewhere."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Sections follow a recognisable structure with headings and a contents list. Figures and tables are unnumbered and untitled, heading levels are inconsistent, and one sentence is broken. Launch-table superscripts cite [49]-[74], which are absent from the 10-item bibliography. [6] is Wikipedia, and [10] is used for two different sources.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"3 February"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[5] f. https://www.qrg.northwestern.edu"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"To compare existing spaceflight mission plans, a wiki sorted directory"},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""}]}]}
